<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/political-ideologies">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Political ideologies are <strong>coherent packages of answers to the big political
    questions</strong> — who should decide, what counts as fairness, what the state is for,
    how much change to risk. The syllabus organises them as families — liberalism,
    conservatism, socialism — each with core principles, internal tensions, and thinkers
    worth quoting accurately. Studying them properly means <strong>steel-manning</strong>
    each: stating each position's best case as its adherents would give it, before
    evaluating. Caricature is not just unfair; it makes you unable to predict what an
    ideology's supporters will do next, which is the practical point of learning them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the map: two axes, then the major families" widget="equationStep" config={ideoCfg} />

  <h2>What an ideology is: a morphology, not a mood</h2>
  <p>
    Ideologies travel in <strong>bundles</strong>: positions on taxes, crime, immigration
    and family correlate because they flow from deeper commitments (equality against
    liberty, universalism against particularism, reason against tradition). Freeden's
    "morphological" method treats each ideology as a structured family of concepts with a
    <strong>core</strong> (what makes it that ideology), <strong>adjacent</strong> and
    <strong>peripheral</strong> concepts that can be rearranged — which is why ideologies
    evolve without dying: liberalism 1850 is not liberalism 2020, but the core persists.
    Every ideology has <strong>wings</strong> — the fights within families are often
    fiercer than fights between them — and ideology is a real cognitive structure:
    positions on one axis moderately predict the rest. Keep the two-axis map (economic
    left-right; social libertarian-authoritarian) in mind throughout, because most
    confusions about ideology are axis errors — calling a free-market libertarian
    "right-wing" about censorship, or a redistributive statist "left-wing" about
    surveillance.
  </p>

  <h2>Liberalism: the ideology of the individual</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Core principles.</strong> Liberalism puts the <strong>individual</strong> —
    rights-bearing, rational, of equal moral worth — first. From that flow its staples:
    <strong>natural rights</strong> (life, liberty, property — claims individuals hold
    against the state, not gifts from it), <strong>government by consent</strong> and
    limited, constitutional rule, the <strong>rule of law</strong>, tolerance of
    difference, and a presumption of liberty in thought, speech and association.
    <strong>Thinkers.</strong> <em>John Locke</em> (Two Treatises, 1689) supplied the
    foundation: individuals hold rights prior to government; government is a trust; when
    it breaches the trust, the people may replace it — the intellectual ammunition of
    every revolution since. <em>Mary Wollstonecraft</em> (A Vindication of the Rights of
    Woman, 1792) pointed the logic at gender: if individuals are rational, the education
    and legal subordination of women is indefensible — liberalism generating feminism
    from its own premises. <em>John Stuart Mill</em> (On Liberty, 1859) supplied the
    boundary rule: the <strong>harm principle</strong> — power over anyone is justified
    only to prevent harm to others; self-regarding choices are sovereign. <em>John
    Rawls</em> (A Theory of Justice, 1971) rebuilt the whole edifice with a thought
    experiment: design society from the <strong>original position</strong>, behind a
    <strong>veil of ignorance</strong>, not knowing where in it you will land. Rational
    self-protection, Rawls argued, yields two principles: equal basic liberties for all,
    and — his signature — the <strong>difference principle</strong>: social and economic
    inequalities are justified only if they benefit the least advantaged.
    <strong>Tensions.</strong> The great one is <em>classical versus social
    liberalism</em>, and it is best seen as a dispute about what freedom <em>is</em>.
    Isaiah Berlin's distinction: <strong>negative liberty</strong> (freedom from
    interference — no one stops you) versus <strong>positive liberty</strong> (freedom
    to — the actual capacity to act, which poverty removes). Classical liberals (and
    <em>Robert Nozick</em>, whose Anarchy, State and Utopia, 1974, defends the minimal
    state and calls redistributive taxation "on a par with forced labor") read freedom
    the first way; social liberals (Rawls at the philosophical end) the second. The
    same dispute drives the modern policy split: is the welfare state liberation
    (giving people real options) or expropriation (taking rightful property)? Note
    that both answers are <em>liberal</em> answers — the individual remains the core;
    only the freedom concept moved.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "liberal" means the same thing in every country. In
  Europe it usually means classical (market-oriented) liberalism; in the US it has come
  to mean social liberalism; on campuses "neoliberal" is an insult aimed at both.
  The ideology's internal axis — negative versus positive liberty — is the map key
  that keeps the uses straight.</p>

  <h2>Conservatism: the ideology of the imperfect</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Core principles.</strong> Conservatism begins in a view of <strong>human
    imperfection</strong>, usually parsed three ways: psychologically (we crave
    belonging and security more than abstraction), morally (we are capable of cruelty;
    order needs enforcing), and intellectually (our reason is fallible and partial).
    From imperfection flow the rest: <strong>tradition</strong> as accumulated,
    tested wisdom — institutions that survived generations embody knowledge no one
    person could reconstruct; <strong>pragmatism</strong> over ideology; an
    <strong>organic society</strong> (society as a living body of interdependent parts,
    not a contract among strangers); <strong>hierarchy and authority</strong> as natural
    and functional; and <strong>property</strong> as security and stake — the
    property-owning person, invested, is a conservative citizen by design.
    <strong>Thinkers.</strong> <em>Edmund Burke</em> (Reflections on the Revolution in
    France, 1790) is the fountainhead — not because he hated change ("a state without
    the means of some change is without the means of its conservation" is his own
    line) but because he argued that inherited institutions carry the wisdom of
    generations and that revolutionary rationalism, starting from a clean slate,
    ends in the guillotine. His "little platoons" — local affections — ground social
    order below the state. <em>Michael Oakeshott</em> (Rationalism in Politics, 1962)
    gave the twentieth-century version: politics as "keeping afloat" on a
    well-found ship, attending to current symptoms rather than sailing for utopias.
    <strong>Tensions.</strong> The sharpest is <em>paternalism versus the New Right</em>.
    <strong>One-Nation conservatism</strong> (Disraeli, from the 1840s-1860s) took the
    organic metaphor seriously: the rich have <em>noblesse oblige</em> — an obligation
    to the poor — and reform (Disraeli's own 1867 franchise extension) conserves order
    by extending stake. The <strong>New Right</strong> (Thatcher, Reagan) fused two
    strands: <strong>neoliberalism</strong> (Hayek's Road to Serfdom, 1944; Friedman —
    roll back the state, unleash markets) and <strong>neoconservatism</strong>
    (strengthen authority, family and nation). The tension is internal and real:
    market radicalism dissolves exactly the traditional institutions (family
    structures, communities, deference) that the conservative half exists to protect —
    which is why New Right governments in practice governed pragmatically, protecting
    some traditions while demolishing others. (Ayn Rand's radical individualism and
    Nozick's minimal state sit at the New Right's libertarian edge — adopted by
    conservatives, yet philosophers first of a liberalism so classical it barely
    recognises itself.)
  </p>

  <h2>Socialism: the ideology of equality</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Core principles.</strong> Socialism's foundation is <strong>equality</strong> —
    not merely legal equality (which liberals had secured) but social and economic:
    formal rights mean little where property concentrates life-chances. From it flow
    <strong>collectivism</strong> (humans are social producers; cooperation outperforms
    competition), <strong>common humanity/fraternity</strong>, <strong>class analysis</strong>
    (society divided by relation to production — the sociology lesson's Marx), and the
    demand for <strong>workers' control</strong> and redistribution. <strong>Thinkers.</strong>
    <em>Marx and Engels</em> (Communist Manifesto, 1848; Capital, 1867) built the
    systematic version: <strong>historical materialism</strong> — history moves through
    stages defined by their economic base, each sowing the contradictions that grow its
    successor; capitalism, by exploiting labour's <strong>surplus value</strong>,
    generates its own "gravediggers" in an increasingly immiserated, concentrated,
    organised proletariat; revolution inaugurates a transitional
    "dictatorship of the proletariat", after which the state — an instrument of class
    rule — "withers away". Whatever one concludes, this is the nineteenth century's
    most consequential social theory. <em>Rosa Luxemburg</em> (Reform or Revolution,
    1900) kept the revolutionary end while attacking Lenin's vanguard: emancipation
    must be "the work of the working class itself" — mass spontaneity, not a
    committee. <em>Sidney and Beatrice Webb</em> founded the <strong>Fabian</strong>
    opposite: socialism by <strong>gradualism</strong> — research, permeation of
    institutions, municipal reform, the ballot box. <em>Anthony Crosland</em> (The
    Future of Socialism, 1956) modernised it: nationalisation was a means, not the
    definition — with a mixed economy delivering growth, the socialist end is
    <strong>equality and social justice</strong>, pursued through welfare, education
    and social provision. <em>Anthony Giddens</em> (The Third Way, 1998) completed the
    arc: the knowledge economy makes the old state-vs-market framing obsolete;
    social democracy should invest in people ("social investment"), balance rights
    with responsibilities. <strong>Tensions.</strong> Revolutionary versus evolutionary
    (Luxemburg versus the Webbs — a split that cost lives in 1917-1921 arguments);
    state ownership versus welfare redistribution (Clause IV of the UK Labour Party,
    whose 1918 common-ownership pledge Blair rewrote in 1995 — the visible marker of
    Crosland's victory); and equality of outcome versus equality of opportunity, which
    splits socialists as sharply as Berlin's liberties split liberals.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> socialism and communism are the same thing, and both
  are the USSR. Socialism is the family; communism is its revolutionary-Marxist wing;
  social democracy is its reformist wing, governing most of Western Europe for
  decades. The Soviet experiment was one branch's governance — as authoritarian as
  it was — and other socialists (Luxemburg among them, from the left) predicted and
  denounced that authoritarianism early.</p>

  <h2>And the others: fascism and anarchism, stated precisely</h2>
  <p>
    The widget's "beyond" families deserve their precise best cases. <strong>Fascism</strong>
    answers "who should decide?" with the nation (or race) as an organic unity above the
    individual: ultra-nationalism, the leader principle, struggle as the engine of
    renewal, anti-liberal and anti-Marxist at once (both put the individual first, in
    its view — one as consumer, one as class member). Its record is the twentieth
    century's catastrophe; its analytic interest is how thoroughly it rejects both
    Enlightenment individualism and class universalism. <strong>Anarchism</strong> answers
    "no one — no state": both Marxist and liberal states, it argues, concentrate
    coercion; voluntary association, mutual aid and federated communes can coordinate
    what states claim only they can (its serious exponents — Proudhon, Kropotkin —
    are the evidence that "order without rulers" is an argument, not a mood).
    Neither is on this syllabus's list, but both sharpen the big three by contrast:
    liberalism individualises, socialism socialises, conservatism historicises —
    fascism totalises, anarchism dissolves.
  </p>

  <h2>How ideologies actually behave</h2>
  <p>
    Four regularities from the research literature. <strong>They travel in bundles</strong>:
    issue positions correlate because they flow from the deep commitments — the bundle
    is the ideology (Converse's classic work on belief systems showed the correlation is
    real but thinner among low-information voters, whose "bundles" are looser —
    ideology is partly expertise). <strong>They evolve</strong>: conservatism adapts to
    what it conserves (monarchy-friendly, then market-friendly); liberalism absorbed
    its own socialist critique; socialism in government became managerial. Freeze-frame
    definitions always go stale — Freeden's morphology is the update mechanism.
    <strong>They are families, not monoliths</strong>: the internal fights (Rawls vs
    Nozick; Burke vs Rand; Luxemburg vs Crosland) are where the interesting arguments
    live, and examiners know it. <strong>They predict</strong>: knowing a person's
    position on one axis moderately predicts the rest of the bundle — and at mass
    scale, the "big sort" of people into like-minded communities (Mason's work on
    mega-identities) is tightening the bundles further.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The psychology under the map.</strong> Moral foundations research (Haidt's
    <em>The Righteous Mind</em>) finds distinct moral matrices — care, fairness,
    loyalty, authority, sanctity, liberty — weighted differently across the spectrum,
    which explains why the same fact pattern activates different intuitions, and why
    argument across families so often fails: the packages differ at the level of what
    counts as a reason. Twin studies suggest ideological temperament is partly
    heritable — the axis map has biology under it, however cultural the contents.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Endings and comebacks.</strong> Fukuyama's "end of history" (1989) argued
    liberal democracy was ideology's terminal form; three decades of populist
    nationalism, one-party techno-authoritarianism and religious politics reopened the
    question — and revived the older insight that ideologies respond to material
    conditions (deindustrialisation and migration moves publics, then parties).
    Meanwhile the big three have each mutated around technology: digital markets
    strained classical liberal property concepts (data as property?), platform
    power strained socialist ownership categories, and tradition-honouring parties
    found themselves championing the most disruptive technologies on offer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Is ideology prior to interest, or a rationalisation
    of it (Marx's own question, turned back on ideology itself)? Can the
    negative/positive liberty dispute be adjudicated empirically — does evidence about
    what actually lifts the worst-off decide Rawls against Nozick, or are they using
    "freedom" and "desert" incommensurably? And as post-material politics (climate,
    identity) reshuffles the old axes, does a third axis — sustainability, sovereignty
    — force the map itself to redraw? The syllabus's bet is the right one to close on:
    the families persist because the questions persist — who decides, who counts, what
    we owe — and each generation re-bundles the answers.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Locke, Two Treatises of Government (1689); Wollstonecraft (1792); Mill, On Liberty (1859); Berlin, Two Concepts of Liberty (1958); Burke, Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790); Oakeshott, Rationalism in Politics (1962); Hayek (1944); Marx & Engels (1848, 1867); Luxemburg (1900); Crosland (1956); Giddens (1998); Rawls (1971); Nozick (1974); Freeden, Ideology: A Very Short Introduction; Haidt (2012); Converse (1964); Mason (2018).</p>

